\section{Discrete Morse functions}
\noindent{}For $K$ an abstract simplicial complex, a map $f:K\tends \reals$ is called a \emph{discrete Morse function} if $\forall~\s^k\in K$
\begin{itemize}
  \item[(i)]$e_1=\lab\lc\t^{k-1}\in K\mid f\lb \tau\rb\geq f\lb \s\rb\rc\rab\leq 1$;
  \item[(ii)]$e_2=\lab\lc\t^{k+1}\in K\mid f\lb \tau\rb\leq f\lb \s\rb\rc\rab\leq 1$.
\end{itemize}
Every function $g$ that \emph{respects dimension} in the sense that for each $\s^{k-1}\subset \t^k\in K$, $g\lb \s\rb<g\lb \t\rb$ is a DMF. On the other hand, a DMF \emph{almost} respects dimension by ensuring that each simplex can have one ``exceptional'' facet and one ``exceptional'' co-face; and in fact, not both. To arrive at a contradiction by assuming that for a face $\s\cup\lc v_1\rc\in K$, where $\s\in K$ and $v_1,v_2\in K_0$, $f\lb \s\rb\geq f\lb \s\cup\lc v_1\rc\rb\geq f\lb \s\cup \lc v_1, v_2\rc\rb$, we have for $\s\cup\lc v_2\rc\in K$, $f\lb \s\cup \lc v_1, v_2\rc\rb>f\lb \s\cup \lc v_2\rc\rb>f\lb\s\rb$, the first strict inequality since the exceptional coface of $\s$ is $\s\cup\lc v_1\rc$ and the second one since the exceptional face of $\s\cup\lc v_1,v_2\rc$ is $\s\cup\lc v_1\rc$. Simplices with exceptions form disjoint pairs of a simplex $\t$ and its facet $\s$, termed \emph{regular pairs}. It encodes an arrow $\s\tends \t$ in the sense that $\s$ can be collapsed onto $\t$. Consectutive collapsings end up in a simplex which can't be collapsed any more, or a simplex without any exception, called a \emph{critical simplex}.

If $n_i$ is the number of critical simplices of dimension $i$ in $K$, $\x=\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^\infty \lb -1\rb^in_i$.

A \emph{discrete vector field} is a disjoint collection of such pairs $\lb \s_i, \t_i\rb$ where for each $i$, $\s_i$ is a facet of $\t_i$. Each pair represents as an arrow $\tends_i$. The disjointness means that each simplex can be a member of at most one pair in a vector field.

If a discrete vector field $\lc\lb\s_i, \t_i\rb\rc_{i\in I}$ is given on a simplicial complex $K$, and $p\in\naturals$, a \emph{$p$-path} is a sequence
$$\s_{i_1}^{p-1}\tends \t_{i_1}^p\geq \s_{i_2}^{p-1}\tends \t_{i_2}^p\geq\s_{i_3}^{p-1}\dots\tends\t_{i_k}^p\geq \s_{i_{k+1}}^{p-1},$$
each arrow in the discrete vector field, and $\s_{i_j}$ is a facet of $\t_{i_{j-1}}$. It's a \emph{cycle} is $\s_1=\s_{k+1},~k\geq 1$, and \emph{acyclic} if it admits no cycle. A critical simplex can only appear as the last simplex in any $p$-path in a discrete vector field.

Regular pairs of a DMF form a discrete vector field. A discrete vector field formed by the regular pairs of a DMF is called a \emph{gradient vector field}.

\begin{thm}
Each gradient vector field is acyclic. On the other hand, each acyclic discrete vector field on a simplicial complex $K$ is induced by some DMF, i.e., is a gradient vector field.
\end{thm}
\noindent{}\emph{Proof.} Given a DMF, in the induced discrete vector field, function values decrease along any $p$-path, since $f\lb \s_{i_j}\rb\geq f\lb \t_{i_j}\rb$ being the only exception for $f\lb \t_{i_j}\rb$, $f\lb \t_{i_j}\rb>f\lb \s_{i_{j+1}}\rb$ for all $j$. Thus, \emph{each gradient vector field is acyclic}. COMPLETE\qed

However, different DMF's on a simplicial complex might induce the same DVF, e.g., if $f$ is a DMF, then so are $e^f$ and $3f$, and all three induce the same DVF. Our primary interest in DVF's lies in their encodings of collapses and deformations-simplifications of a simplicial complex. A DMF represents a convenient but not unique way of encoding a DVF.
\begin{prop}
Suppose the critical simplices of an acyclic DVF on $K$ form a subcomplex $L\leq K$. Then $\exists$ a collapse $K\tends L$, and so, $K\simeq L$.
\end{prop}
\noindent{}For $\s\in K\setminus L$, we claim the existence of a regular pair $\lb \s,\t\rb$ where $\s$ is a \emph{free face} of $\t$, whence we can perform an elementary collapse, and proceed using the same claim on the resulting complex. To prove the claim, let $n$ denote the maximal dimension of a simplex in $K\setminus L$. There exist $n$-path(s) in the DVF (of which we take the maximal), and $\s\tends\t$ be its first regular pair. Now, $\s\leq \t$, and if $\s$ were a facet of some other simplex $\t'$ in $K\setminus L$, then we can extend the $n$-path as $\t'\geq \s\tends\t\geq\dots$, contradicting its maximality. On the other hand, if $\s$ were a facet of some simplex $\t'$ in $L$, then $\s\in L$ as $L$ is a subcomplex, a contradiction.\qed
\section{Morse homology}
\noindent{}An \emph{oriented $p$-path} from $\s_1^{p-1}$ to $\s_{k+1}^{p-1}$ is a $p$-path consisting of oriented simplices, such that for each $j$ the orientation induced by $\t_j$ on its facets (i) does not match $\s_j$ and (ii) matches $\s_{j+1}$. One could say that the simplices $\t_{i_j}$ in an oriented $p$-path are oriented consistently along the path.

Let us start with a simplicial complex $K$, an gradient vector field on it, and $\bF$ an (algebraic) field to provide efficient algebraic constructions. For each $i$, let $n_i$ denote $\#$ critical $i$-simplices. For $p\in\naturals\cup\lc 0\rc$, a \emph{Morse $p$-chain} is a formal sum $\sum_{i=1}^{n_p}\l_i\s_i^p;~\l_i\in\bF,~\s_i^p$ an oriented critical $p$-simplex in $K$ for each $i$. The module of all such Morse $p$-chains is the \emph{$p$-dimensional Morse chain group} $\C_p\cong \bF^{n_p}$. Now, we have to define the boundary map. Given an oriented critical $p$-simplex $\t$, let $\dt\lb \t\rb$ be its collection of facets with induced orientation. For each $\s\in\C_{p-1}$, let $\a_{\t,\s}\coloneq \displaystyle\sum_{\s'\in\dt\lb \t\rb}\#\lc\text{oriented paths from $\s'$ to $\s$}\rc$. Since we are considering oriented simplices, $\a_{\t,\s}$ counts paths different from those $\a_{\t,-\s}$ counts. For $\t\in\C_p$, we define $\dl_p\t\coloneq \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n_{p-1}}\lb\a_{\t, \s_i}-\a_{\t, -\s_i}\rb\s_i$. The oriented paths constituting the boundary map model how arrows stretch the boundary of a critical $p$-simplex towards critical $\lb p-1\rb$-simplices. As we have got a Morse chain complex $\dots\xrightarrow{\dl}\C_n\xrightarrow{\dl}\C_{n-1}\xrightarrow{\dl}\dots \xrightarrow{\dl}\C_1\xrightarrow{\dl}\C_0\xrightarrow{\dl}0$ with $\dl^2=0$, we can define \emph{Morse homology} of a gradient vector field on $K$ as $\cH\lb K;\bF\rb=\ker \dl_p/\im \dl_{p+1}$. This is isomorphic to standard simplical and singular homologies. One way to see this is that nowhere do we change the homotopy type of the complex.
\paragraph*{Weak Morse inequalities.}For each $p$, $\#$ critical $p$-simplices is greater than or equal to the corresponding Betti number: $n_p\geq \betti_p$.

