\subsubsection{Collapsing and Morse chain groups}
It is easy to check that the torus can be serially collapsed on to the simplex colored green. Let's call it $\t$, which is homotopic to the entire torus. We see that $x$ is a critical vertex, $a$ and $b$ are critical edges, and $\t$ is the critical triangle. The Morse chain complex is of the form $\dots\tends 0\tends \bF\tends \bF^2\tends \bF\tends 0$. We need now to compute the boundary function.
\subsubsection{Boundary functions and homology}
We see that $\dt\lb \t\rb$ has 3 edges, which we name as $\t_1, \t_2$ and $\t_3$ as in the figure. From $\t_1$, there is one oriented 2-path to critical edge $-\la a\ra$ (marked in blue) and another oriented 2-path to critical edge $-\la b\ra$ (marked in red). From $\t_2$, we have such two oriented 2-paths to $\la a\ra$ and $\la b\ra$ respectively marked in green and yellow. No oriented 2-path from $\t_3$ end up in a critical edge. Thus, $\dl\lb \t\rb=-\la a\ra-\la b \ra+\la a \ra+\la b \ra=0$. Similarly, $\dl a=\dl b=0$, so $\dl_1\equiv 0$. This gives the Morse chain complex:
$$\dots\tends 0\tends \bF\xrightarrow{\dl_2\equiv 0} \bF^2\xrightarrow{\dl_1\equiv 0} \bF\tends 0.$$
Thus, $\cH_0\lb K\rb\cong \cH_2\lb K\rb \cong \bF$ and $\cH_1\lb K\rb\cong\bF^2$.
